## Authentication

`go-cli-project` sends {{ if .Computed.oauth }}an access token{{ else }}an API key{{ end }} to its API. It uses the first of these sources that gives one:

1. The environment variable `GO_CLI_PROJECT_{{ .Computed.variable }}`
{{- if .Computed.oauth }}
2. The tokens that `go-cli-project auth login` stores
{{- else if .Computed.stored }}
2. The API key that `go-cli-project auth login` stores
{{- end }}
{{ if .Computed.stored }}3{{ else }}2{{ end }}. The output of `credentialCommand` in `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/go-cli-project/config.yaml`, or in
   `~/.config/go-cli-project/config.yaml` when `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is not set

`go-cli-project auth status` shows which source gives the {{ .Computed.label }}. It does not show the {{ .Computed.label }}.

Use the environment variable in CI and in scripts.

[docs/authentication.md](docs/authentication.md) tells how the code works, and why.
{{- if .Computed.oauth }}

### Log in

`go-cli-project auth login` opens a browser, where you log in at the issuer. The CLI uses the OAuth
authorization code flow with PKCE, and the issuer sends the login back to a server on `127.0.0.1`.

If the machine has no browser, for example in an SSH session, `auth login` shows a URL and a code. Open
the URL on any device and type the code. `auth login --device` does the same on a machine with a browser.
If the environment variable `BROWSER` is set, `go-cli-project` opens the browser that it names.

```shell
go-cli-project auth login            # log in in a browser
go-cli-project auth login --device   # log in with a code on another device
go-cli-project auth status           # show the source, the account and the expiry time
go-cli-project auth token            # print the access token, for scripts
go-cli-project auth logout           # revoke the refresh token and remove the tokens
```

`auth login` keeps the tokens in the OS credential store: the macOS Keychain, the Windows Credential
Manager, or the Linux Secret Service. If the machine has no credential store, it keeps them in
`credentials.json`, in the same folder as `config.yaml`. Only you can read that file. When the access
token expires, `go-cli-project` gets a new one with the refresh token.
{{- else if .Computed.stored }}

### Store the API key

```shell
go-cli-project auth login                        # paste the API key
printf '%s' "$KEY" | go-cli-project auth login   # or send it on stdin
go-cli-project auth logout                       # remove the stored API key
```

`auth login` keeps the API key in the OS credential store: the macOS Keychain, the Windows Credential
Manager, or the Linux Secret Service. If the machine has no credential store, it keeps the key in
`credentials.json`, in the same folder as `config.yaml`. Only you can read that file.
{{- end }}
{{- if .Computed.stored }}

`GO_CLI_PROJECT_CREDENTIAL_STORE=file` makes `go-cli-project` use only `credentials.json`. The e2e tests
use it, so that they do not change your keychain.
{{- end }}

### Read the {{ .Computed.label }} from a password manager

`credentialCommand` runs through `sh`, and `go-cli-project` uses what the command prints. With it, the
{{ .Computed.label }} stays in your password manager:

```yaml
credentialCommand: op read op://Private/go-cli-project/{{ if .Computed.oauth }}token{{ else }}api-key{{ end }}
```

`go-cli-project` reads `credentialCommand` only from your own config file, and never from a file in a
repository.
{{- if .Computed.oauth }}

### Set up the OAuth client

Before the first login, set these constants. Each environment variable replaces its constant, for example
for a staging issuer:

| Constant | File | Environment variable |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `issuerURL` | `internal/credential/issuer.go` | `GO_CLI_PROJECT_OAUTH_ISSUER_URL` |
| `clientID` | `internal/credential/issuer.go` | `GO_CLI_PROJECT_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID` |
| `apiURL` | `internal/credential/api_url.go` | `GO_CLI_PROJECT_API_URL` |

`go-cli-project` finds the endpoints in the OIDC discovery document of the issuer, at
`/.well-known/openid-configuration`. Register the client at the issuer with these settings:

- A public client, with no client secret. A CLI cannot keep a secret (RFC 8252).
- The redirect URL `http://127.0.0.1/callback`, with any port.
- The authorization code grant, the refresh token grant, and the device authorization grant.

`scopes` in `internal/credential/issuer.go` lists the scopes. At most issuers, `offline_access` gives
the refresh token.

The stored tokens belong to the host of the API URL. If `GO_CLI_PROJECT_API_URL` names another server,
`go-cli-project` does not send the tokens to that server.
{{- else if .Computed.stored }}

### Set the API URL

Before the first login, set `apiURL` in `internal/credential/api_url.go`. The environment variable
`GO_CLI_PROJECT_API_URL` replaces it, for example for a staging server.

The stored API key belongs to the host of the API URL. If `GO_CLI_PROJECT_API_URL` names another server,
`go-cli-project` does not send the stored API key to that server.
{{- end }}
